      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The originalist and living constitutionalist interpretations of the Second Amendment are two opposing perspectives on the meaning of the amendment. Originalists, such as Justice Scalia, adhere to the text and historical context at the time of its ratification, interpreting the amendment to protect an individual right to keep and bear arms. In contrast, living constitutionalists, like Justice Blackmun, view the amendment as a constitutional principle that evolves with the surrounding legal and social context, allowing courts to interpret and adapt its scope. Stovall v. Denver and District Council of Trade Unions, 677 F.2d _Use JELLIK_?, provide seminal rulings on these opposing viewpoints.",
