    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but could you go deeper? I'd like to understand the actual legal reasoning. Specifically, what are the core philosophical pillars or evidentiary standards that Originalists rely on to justify their method, and how do Living Constitutionalists respond to those specific points? Try to provide the strongest version of each argument.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalists rely on several core philosophical pillars to justify their interpretation, including textualism, historical preservationism, and the Federalist Papers' discussion of a 'military necessity' clause. They also argue that the Second Amendment's use of the word 'bear' implies that it refers to armed self-defense rather than collective defense. \n\nOn the other hand, Living Constitutionalists respond that textualism and historical preservationism are overly narrow or misleading approaches, as the surrounding context of the time period can provide a broader interpretation of the amendment. They argue that the Founding Fathers and the broader historical context of the 18th century imply a right to collective self-defense as well. The transcript by Antonin Scalia in the case of District of Columbia v. Heller, 554 U.S. __ (2008), highlights these contrasting perspectives on the Second Amendment's meaning.",
      "source": "target",
